{"study_accession":"PRJNA341951","secondary_study_accession":"SRP086613","title":"CD161+ Tconv and Treg share transcriptome and display a migratory phenotype which is modified at the inflamed site","description":"Regulatory T cells (Treg) are a cornerstone of immune regulation. Recent evidence indicates that human Treg show plasticity in specific settings. A subpopulation of Treg expressing CD161, a marker associated with T helper (Th)17 cells, have T effector -like characteristics and are enriched at sites of autoimmune inflammation. Here we used RNAseq to confirm the effector-like signature of CD161+ Treg and demonstrated a shared transcriptional signature between CD161+ Treg and CD161+ conventional T cells (Tconv). Pathway analysis suggested that CD161+ T cells have a migratory phenotype, expressing high levels of CCR9 and integrin α4β7, markers associated with gut homing. In response to all-trans retinoic acid, CD161+ T cells expressed higher levels of CCR9 and integrin α4β7 than CD161- T cells. Our data suggest that blood CD161+ T cells may have adopted gut homing properties upon retinoic acid exposure. In contrast to their peripheral counterparts, CD161+ T cells from the site of autoimmune arthritis have a diminished gut homing phenotype and blunted response to retinoic acid. In health, the TCRβ repertoires of CD161+ and CD161- T cells showed limited overlap whereas there is clear overlap in T cell clones from synovial fluid of autoimmune arthritis patients. We therefore propose that CD161+ and CD161- T cells are largely distinct populations in the healthy immune system but that the inflamed site creates an environment where CD161 levels in T cells can be altered, potentially contributing to disease pathogenesis. Overall design: Transcriptional profilling of CD161+ and CD161- Tconv, and CD161+ and CD161- Tconv from blood of healthy adult controls. The raw files provided are BAM files, and the two associated raw files are merged into one BAM file for each sample. Note: Sample 2 CD161+ Treg was excluded due to low cDNA yield","pubmed":"28321213","geo_series":"","n_samples":15,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN05730779","study_accession":"PRJNA341951","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample 1_CD161+ Treg","description":"Sample 1_CD161+ Treg","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2302911","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":2,"first_public":"2017-03-30","attributes":[["source_name","PBMC_CD161+ Treg"],["subject status","Healthy Adult Control"],["cell_type","Peripheral Blood Mononuclear Cells (PBMCs)"],["cell_subtype","CD161+ Treg"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR4174931","experiment_accession":"SRX2140250","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":27407868,"base_count":2670797950,"first_public":"2017-03-30","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM2302911: Sample 1_CD161+ Treg Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"},{"run_accession":"SRR4174932","experiment_accession":"SRX2140250","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":1530556,"base_count":83066750,"first_public":"2017-03-30","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM2302911: Sample 1_CD161+ Treg Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[]},{"sample_accession":"SAMN05730781","study_accession":"PRJNA341951","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample 4_CD161+ Treg","description":"Sample 4_CD161+ Treg","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2302922","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":2,"first_public":"2017-03-30","attributes":[["source_name","PBMC_CD161+ Treg"],["subject status","Healthy Adult Control"],["cell_type","Peripheral Blood Mononuclear Cells (PBMCs)"],["cell_subtype","CD161+ Treg"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR4174953","experiment_accession":"SRX2140261","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":15606659,"base_count":1522122050,"first_public":"2017-03-30","ex